+# Send pickup reminders by SMS
+
+For volunteers considering a cheaper channel for pickup reminders. The pantry keeps SMS because the email trial
+saw more missed pickups and wasted fresh food; saving the SMS bill did not justify those losses.
+
+## Context
+
+The pantry tried email pickup reminders from April to June 2025. As reported in
+[issue 52](../../notes/issue-52.md), the provider's report showed that 34% of reminder emails were never opened.
+Missed pickups rose from 6% to 13% of pantry orders. Each missed pickup leaves a packed bag, whose fresh food goes
+in the bin by closing time.
+
+The pantry lead reported that many members share one phone in the family and never check email, but see SMS.
+The pantry switched back to SMS on July 1, 2025; missed pickups were back to 6% by August.
+
+Issue 52 proposed email again to save the reported SMS cost of €40 per month. It was the third such proposal that
+year, and the maintainer closed it with the decision to keep SMS. The tradeoff is the reminder bill against reaching
+members in time for pickup and avoiding wasted food.
+
+## Options
+
+- **Email**: free to send, saving the reported €40 monthly SMS bill. Rejected because the trial left 34% of reminder
+  emails unopened and saw missed pickups rise to 13%, with fresh food discarded from uncollected bags.
+- **SMS**: costs money to send, but reaches members who do not check email. Chosen after missed pickups returned
+  to 6% following its restoration.
+
+## Decision
+
+Send pickup reminders by SMS. Reaching members and reducing missed pickups and food waste outweigh the reported
+€40 monthly saving from email. The current implementation selects SMS in
+[`larder/reminders.py`](../../larder/reminders.py).
+
+## Consequences
+
+The pantry continues paying for SMS reminders. A future proposal to change channels needs to address member reach,
+missed pickups and food waste as well as sending cost; email's lower price was already weighed in this decision.
